# Client config. ONLY these two may ever be VITE_ vars.
#
# Vite inlines VITE_* verbatim into dist/assets/*.js, so anything here is
# public. The Supabase anon key is designed for that — it identifies the project
# and Row Level Security guards the data. A model-provider key is NOT, and must
# never appear in this file. Those live as Edge Function secrets (below).
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://tpsgpghpqgzmgjlshdzj.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# --- Server side: Edge Function secrets, NOT this file ----------------------
#
# Set with the Supabase CLI, then deploy the function:
#
#   npx supabase secrets set \
#     ALLOWED_EMAILS=you@example.com \
#     COMPAT_MODEL=grok-4.5 \
#     COMPAT_API_KEY=... \
#     COMPAT_BASE_URL=https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1 \
#     ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-... \
#     MODEL=claude-opus-5
#
#   npx supabase functions deploy engine
#
# Provider order is ENGINE_ORDER, default "compat,anthropic": the OpenAI-
# compatible gateway leads, Anthropic catches its failures. Set
# ENGINE_ORDER=anthropic,compat to lead with Claude instead. Either provider can
# be omitted entirely — the chain just gets shorter. The original FALLBACK_*
# names are still read as aliases for COMPAT_*, so flipping the order needs no
# secrets to be re-set.
#
# ALLOWED_EMAILS matters: Supabase Auth allows public signup by default, so
# without it anyone who signs up can spend your model budget.
